Caption buttons for what a window may do, as Windows 11 draws them.

Every window got Minimize, Maximize and Close, and all three worked: a
Properties box or a message box could be maximised, its few controls
stretched across the screen.  ctwm reads the Motif hints only for the
decorations, and fullzoom() zoomed any window.

What a window may do is now worked out the way Windows decides it:

  maximise -- not when its size is fixed (the same minimum and maximum size
              in WM_NORMAL_HINTS, which is how GTK marks a dialog that is
              not resizable) or its _MOTIF_WM_HINTS leave the maximise
              function out;
  minimise -- not when it is a dialog (transient for another window) or its
              Motif hints leave the minimise function out.

With FluentTitles, a caption button for something the window may not do is
drawn greyed, gets no highlight under the pointer and does nothing when
pressed; when the window may do neither, Minimize and Maximize are not drawn
at all and only Close remains.  fullzoom() refuses such a window too, so a
double click on its title, a key binding or an EWMH request do not maximise
it either; a window already zoomed can still be restored.  The answer is
kept per window and worked out again when WM_NORMAL_HINTS or
_MOTIF_WM_HINTS change.

